<h2>A court built for the child</h2>
<p>Section 28 of the POCSO Act requires the State Government, in consultation with the Chief Justice of the High Court, to designate a Court of Session in each district as the Special Court for the Act. Under the child-friendly justice framework, many districts — including the Delhi districts — now have exclusive POCSO courts that try nothing else. Section 33(1) then removes a whole procedural stage: the Special Court may take cognizance directly on a complaint or a police report, without committal proceedings before a Magistrate. The trial therefore starts closer to the filing of the chargesheet than in an ordinary sessions case.</p>

<h2>The statutory calendar</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Provision</th><th>What the Act requires</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Cognizance</td><td>Section 33(1)</td><td>Special Court takes cognizance directly; no committal needed.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Child&rsquo;s evidence</td><td>Section 35(1)</td><td>Evidence of the child to be recorded within 30 days of the Special Court taking cognizance; reasons for any delay to be recorded.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Completion of trial</td><td>Section 35(2)</td><td>Trial to be completed, as far as possible, within one year of taking cognizance.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The thirty-day rule reflects a simple forensic truth: a child&rsquo;s memory, confidence and willingness to speak all fade with time, and every month of delay multiplies the opportunities for pressure on the family. The one-year direction is qualified by &ldquo;as far as possible&rdquo;, but it is not decorative — it is the benchmark against which adjournment requests and listing gaps are judged, and the foundation for applications seeking expedition.</p>

<h2>How the courtroom itself changes</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Frequent breaks.</strong> Section 33(3) requires the Special Court to permit frequent breaks for the child during the trial, so that testimony is taken at the child&rsquo;s pace rather than the board&rsquo;s.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>No aggressive questioning.</strong> Section 33(6) obliges the court to ensure that the child is not aggressively questioned or subjected to character assassination. Cross-examination questions are put through the court in a child-appropriate form.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The child does not face the accused.</strong> Section 36 requires the court to ensure the child does not see the accused while testifying, using screens, one-way arrangements or video means — while preserving the accused&rsquo;s right to hear the testimony and instruct counsel.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>One appearance, not many.</strong> Section 33(5) directs that the child not be called repeatedly to testify, and Section 37 makes the trial in camera, in the presence of a parent or a person the child trusts.</div>
</div>

<h2>Where the clock actually slips</h2>
<p>In practice, three pressure points account for most delay. First, forensic science laboratory reports — particularly electronic and biological evidence — often arrive months after cognizance, and prosecutors are reluctant to close evidence without them. Second, witnesses turn hostile or move away, prompting repeated summons and bailable warrants. Third, mid-trial applications — for alteration of charge, further investigation or recalling witnesses — restart arguments that were thought closed. None of these is unique to POCSO, but each collides with a statute that promised speed.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>For the prosecution and the victim&rsquo;s family:</strong> an application invoking Section 35, seeking day-to-day or time-bound recording of the child&rsquo;s evidence, is the direct statutory remedy; the High Court&rsquo;s administrative and judicial oversight can be sought where listing itself is the obstacle.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>For the defence:</strong> the same calendar cuts the other way. Where the accused is in custody and the trial has not progressed for reasons not attributable to him, prolonged incarceration becomes a substantial bail consideration — argued on the record of dates, not rhetoric.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>For both sides:</strong> keeping an accurate order-sheet chronology — who sought which adjournment, when — is the single most useful habit, because every expedition or bail argument ultimately rests on it.</div>
</div>

<div class="note">The POCSO calendar is a promise the system does not always keep, but it is far from toothless: it frames bail arguments, expedition applications and appellate scrutiny alike. Families of victims should know the timeline the law sets for them; accused persons should know that the same timeline is the measure of how long pre-trial custody can fairly run. Specific advice in a pending matter should always be taken on the actual record of that case.</div>
